# Engineering standard

Write for a new collaborator. Be ready to say how new work honors these.

- **SRP** — one reason to change per module. Split a god-module before adding capability.
  Keep `features` / `components` / `lib` / `app` boundaries. One administration concern per
  file, composed by an index.
- **DRY, but not too DRY.** Share an interface when things differ. No universal helper that
  collapses unrelated domains.
- **Dependency inversion** — an obvious second consumer stands alone; callers depend on an
  injectable seam so tests inject fakes.
- **Open/closed** — adding a case is add + register, touch nothing else.
- **Self-documenting names.** Name by meaning. Never comment a variable — fix the name.
- **Comment density.** A comment earns its place with a measurement or a rejected alternative,
  not by narrating the code. Two lines, not nine.
- **Docstrings** (when needed): one short line of WHAT it does (lead with a verb); what it
  takes; what it returns including the absent case. No "because", no algorithm, no history.
- **Altitude.** Fix at the right depth. No bandaid on shared infra; don't build the maximal
  engine for one case. Freeze an abstraction after a second implementation proves it, unless
  the maintainer called out plumbing every part touches — then build it properly now.
- **Value before coverage.** The cheap approach that covers the real case beats the expensive
  one that covers hypotheticals. Grep the code and the open issues before writing.
- **Measure before optimizing.** Don't ship a win that measures ~1×.
- A guard must call the check's own code. A self-test that re-derives the check is a second
  implementation and they drift.
